m previous layer\n```\n\n### 4. **The Three Main Kernels and Their Metrics**\n\n| Kernel | Launches | Total Time | Per-Instance | Purpose |\n|--------|----------|-----------|--------------|---------|\n| `poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel` | 25 | 231ms | 9.2ms avg (0.7-45ms) | Hash matrix rows + tree within query_stride leaves |\n| `poseidon2_strided_compress_layer_kernel` | 64 | 75ms | ~1.2ms avg | Merkle tree layer compression |\n| `calculate_zero_hash` | 584 | 109ms | ~0.19ms avg | Placeholder hashes for padding |\n| `stack_columns_kernel` | 11 | 16ms | ~1.5ms avg | Stacking traces into unified matrix |\n\n### 5. **One Merkle Tree Per Segment, Sequential Construction**\n\n**Key Finding**: There is **ONE Merkle tree per stacked segment**, not per AIR or per sub-group.\n\n**File**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs` (lines 304-317)\n- Iterate AIRs and their segments\n- **For each segment**: Call `stacked_commit()` once → creates 1 Merkle tree\n- Sequential: Each segment processed independently\n\n**Question 2 Answer**: **One Merkle tree per segment**. The StackedLayout handles grouping all traces of that segment (sorted by height) into one stacked matrix. All columns of that matrix → single Reed-Solomon codeword matrix → single Merkle tree.\n\n**Question 3 Answer**: **Sequential**. The loop in `prove_stacked_opening_reduction_gpu()` iterates segments one at a time. Each segment's Merkle tree is built before moving to the next. However, multiple segments could theoretically be batched at the WHIR opening stage (proven by `stacked_per_commit: Vec<...>`).\n\n### 6. **Memory Layout: How stacked_commit Processes Height Groups**\n\n**File**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/stark-backend/src/prover/stacked_pcs.rs` (lines 144-203)\n\n```\nStackedLayout::new(l_skip, log_stacked_height, sorted_meta)\n├─ Input: traces sorted descending by log_height\n├─ Algorithm: Greedy column packing\n│  └─ For each matrix m_i with width w_i:\n│ 