he actual performance target. We're at 1297ms for APC 300 and need to hit the 2x improvement threshold. The baseline at APC 0 is 2142ms, so the current ratio is only 1.65x—we're not quite there yet.\n\nTo reach 2x, APC 300 needs to drop below 1071ms, which means I need to find another 226ms in savings. Looking at the component breakdown, there's still room to optimize, though it's getting tight.\n\nLet me evaluate the highest-impact options. Multi-stream GKR proof tree building with overlapped GPU work doesn't yield much since the CPU portion is already minimal. The Merkle tree hash calculation is a bigger target at 109ms total, though it's distributed across many trees.\n\nThe most promising angle is batching the MLE round kernels—instead of launching separate operations for precompute, monomial conversion, sumcheck, interpolation, and folding per round, I could fuse adjacent kernels together to cut launch overhead and memory traffic, potentially saving 20-30ms.\n\nLooking at the specific kernel sequence, there are roughly 60-80 launches per round. If I could consolidate down to 10-15 launches through batching, that's about 9ms saved just from reduced launch overhead across all 14 rounds. The real win though is fusing precompute_logup_combinations directly into the monomial kernel—instead of writing combinations to memory and reading them back, I'd compute them on-the-fly, eliminating over 100 memory transactions per round.\n\nBut the math doesn't work out. The precompute output is only a few KB, so even across 14 rounds and 2 segments, the memory savings are negligible at 1TB/s bandwidth. The fusion mainly buys me launch overhead reduction, which tops out around 9ms—not enough to explain the gap.\n\nI need to look at this differently. Let me correlate the nsight kernel metrics directly with the NVTX timing ranges to see where the actual bottleneck is. The problem is nsight doesn't map cleanly to those timing spans, so I'm checking the NVTX data to find the culprit