C 300 are small and go through the monomial path, so fusing precompute into monomial would capture most of the gains while leaving the batch_mle path unchanged. This partial fusion trades some optimization for reduced complexity.\n\nNow I'm wondering if there's a different angle—looking at how the MLE rounds themselves interact with the fold operation. Each round evaluates the sumcheck polynomials, observes results, samples a challenge, then folds the MLE evaluations using that challenge before interpolating for the next round.\n\nThe key insight is that fold is the only step producing data for the next round, while the evaluation step only needs the matrices after folding is complete. So I can't overlap evaluation with fold directly, but I could pipeline CPU work with GPU execution—specifically, while round N's fold kernel runs on the GPU, I could have the CPU pre-build the descriptor arrays for round N+1 using pre-computed trace metadata. Once fold finishes, the descriptors are ready and I can immediately launch the next evaluation kernels, hiding the CPU descriptor preparation latency.\n\nLooking at the actual timing: the current flow has a ~0.5-1ms gap between GPU fold and CPU descriptor building per round, which across 14 rounds and 2 segments adds up to roughly 14-28ms of potential savings. The tricky part is that the descriptor arrays depend on fold state—which matrices are active, their new heights, and their new pointer locations in the ping-pong buffers. So I need to figure out what can actually be pre-computed independently of the fold operation itself.\n\nThe descriptor building really breaks down into two pieces: structural metadata like which traces participate and their types, which stays fixed, and dynamic metadata like matrix pointers and eq_xi values that shift after each fold. If I pre-compute all the structural metadata upfront for every round in the MLE sequence, I could eliminate that computation from the per-round loop entirely, leaving only a