ly. There's a single stacked_commit call for all traces, but the NTT and Merkle tree operations work on the full stacked matrix, which has around 812 columns.\n\nI'm wondering if my column estimate is off. The tracing output should log the actual stacked matrix dimensions, but I'd need to run the benchmark with logging enabled to see it. That's a 5+ minute investment though. Instead, let me work backwards from what I know: with 811M total cells and 106,842 columns, I can estimate the stacked matrix width.\n\nActually, I'm realizing the stacking mechanism is more nuanced than I initially thought. It vertically packs columns of different heights into the same stacked column—so if a stacked column has height H, shorter traces get zero-padded to fit. This means many small traces at APC 300 would be packed together, resulting in fewer but taller stacked columns overall. The width calculation groups columns by height and counts how many fit in each group, but the actual packing could reduce the final width significantly.\n\nLet me trace through the stacking logic to understand how columns are actually combined. I'm working through the stacking parameters and realizing I need the actual WHIR PCS values to compute the exact matrix dimensions, so let me move forward with a concrete optimization approach instead of getting stuck on the calculations.\n\nLooking at the APC 300 STARK profile, LogUp GKR dominates at 534ms, and the real bottleneck is the GKR input evaluation which runs at 300ms wall-clock despite only needing 64ms of actual GPU time—the 236ms gap is driven by one stream's large AIR computation overwhelming the others.\n\nThe key insight is that the largest AIR (height ~65536) has a single kernel taking 10-50ms that serializes its entire stream, so splitting that AIR's work across multiple parallel streams could let them finish together instead of waiting for the dominant one.\n\nSplitting the kernel into sub-kernels on different streams won't help with memory bandwidth since the total throughput stays the same, but there's a potential win if the sub-kernels fit better in the L2 cache (72MB) and avoid thrashing the 40MB intermediates buffer.\n\nI'm exploring a two-level multi-streaming approach where instead of 8 sequential streams each processing AIRs one-by-one, I could use a work-stealing or dynamic scheduling model to keep all GPU resources busy and prevent any single large AIR from becoming the bottleneck.\n\nThe key insight is that large AIRs already saturate the GPU, so they don't benefit from splitting or multi-streaming—but small AIRs do. So maybe the right approach is to process large AIRs sequentially on the main stream while distributing small AIRs across worker streams, though I'm realizing this might already be what's happening at the current threshold.\n\nActually, looking at this more carefully: the current approach assigns all AIRs to worker threads, which means large ones block their assigned thread. If I separated them—running large AIRs sequentially on the main stream and small ones across 8 worker streams—I'd avoid blocking any worker. But the GPU would be fully occupied during the large AIR phase, so there's no room for the small AIRs to overlap and run concurrently. The large AIRs use all 128 SMs, leaving nothing for the worker streams to use.\n\nSo overlapping them isn't feasible without advanced techniques like CUDA MPS. Instead, I'm going to focus on multi-streaming the kernel launches within each MLE round's height group processing. Right now these launch sequentially—each height group triggers about 4-6 kernels in order across 28 groups per round. If I could launch them on different streams, I might get better occupancy and hide some latency.\n\nThe real opportunity is that most height groups at APC 300 have tiny traces with kernels that only use a handful of blocks. Currently they're launched one after another, leaving the GPU mostly idle. By spreading launches across multiple streams, I could keep more SMs occupied by running several small kernels concurrently. The larger kernels probably won't overlap since they saturate the GPU anyway, but the small ones should benefit significantly. Looking at the numbers, the precompute and monomial evaluation paths together take around 88ms of GPU time, so there's real potential to compress that if I can eliminate the sequential launch overhead.\n\nThe tricky part is whether multi-streaming actually fits into the MLE round structure. Each round needs polynomial evaluations first, then the CPU samples a challenge, then we fold the results. If I multi-stream the kernel launches in step 1, I'd need to make sure all the results are ready before the CPU can observe and sample. The kernels would finish faster with better overlap, but I still have to wait for everything to complete before moving to the CPU work.\n\nLooking at the actual reduction pattern: the batch_mle kernel accumulates results across all traces into a single polynomial value. If I split this across streams, I'd get partial sums that need combining—that's an extra synchronization step. A cleaner approach might be to multi-stream just the precompute and monomial evaluation stages (which don't do reduction), then feed all those outputs into the existing reduction kernel on the main stream.\n\nBut I'm realizing the math doesn't work out as well as I initially thought. Those individual kernels are tiny—42 microseconds for precompute, around 100 for monomial—so with 4 streams handling 7 groups each, I'm only saving maybe 4 milliseconds from the overlap, not the 44 milliseconds I was hoping for.\n\nLet me think through this more carefully. The 88 milliseconds is spread across all 791 precompute calls, but in a single round there are only about 28 groups. Each round's precompute takes roughly 1.2 milliseconds, and with 4 streams I could cut that to 0.3 milliseconds—saving about 0.9 milliseconds per round. Across 14 rounds and 2 segments, that's around 25 milliseconds per variant. But the variants run sequentially, so I need to reconsider whether I'm actually doubling that savings or if there's something else I'm missing about how the computation flows.\n\nLooking at the actual breakdown per round per segment: the false variant precompute is about 0.7 milliseconds, the true variant is 1.2 milliseconds, and the monomial operations add another 2.8 milliseconds, totaling 4.7 milliseconds sequentially. With multi-streaming across 4 streams, I can compress each of those down to roughly 0.18, 0.29, and 0.7 milliseconds respectively, bringing the total to 1.17 milliseconds. That's a savings of 3.53 milliseconds per round per segment, which scales to about 99 milliseconds across all 14 rounds and 2 segments.\n\nActually, let me recalculate this more carefully. I'm seeing 791 total calls of the true variant precompute, which at 42 microseconds each is 33 milliseconds total. Dividing by the 28 calls per round per segment gives 1.176 milliseconds per round per segment. With 4 streams overlapping, that drops to 294 microseconds, saving about 0.882 milliseconds per round per segment, or roughly 24.7 milliseconds across the full benchmark. The false variant follows a similar pattern with its own timing breakdown.\n\nFor the monomial kernels, I'm looking at combined times around 53 milliseconds, which translates to about 1.89 milliseconds per round per segment. Multi-streaming brings that down to 0.47 milliseconds, yielding nearly 40 milliseconds in savings. Adding up all three kernel types—precompute true, precompute false, and monomial—I'm getting roughly 79 milliseconds total savings from parallelizing across streams. That would cut the MLE rounds time nearly in half, from 158 milliseconds down to 79 milliseconds.\n\nBut I need to be realistic about whether we actually achieve that perfect 4x speedup. Stream overhead, GPU scheduler contention, memory bandwidth limits, and synchronization costs will eat into the gains. The kernels are tiny