heck evaluations, I'd save 3ms per round across 14 rounds, which comes to about 42ms total.\n\nNow I'm thinking through whether that overlap is actually feasible. Both paths launch similar-sized kernels across the same set of traces, so they'd be competing for GPU resources. At APC 300 with small traces, each kernel might only use 10 SMs out of the GPU's 128, which means both could run concurrently without too much contention. The realistic savings would probably be in the 20-40ms range if the GPU can handle both workloads simultaneously.\n\nThe cleanest approach would be to restructure the evaluation function to run logup on one stream and zerocheck on another stream in parallel, then synchronize at the end. The tricky part is managing GPU buffer allocations when both paths are running concurrently.\n\nActually, I realize the codebase already handles this pattern with `cudaStreamPerThread` — each OS thread gets its own CUDA stream automatically. So I can spawn two threads, one for logup evaluation and one for zerocheck evaluation, let them run in parallel, then join and collect the results.\n\nThe shared state like `TraceCtx` and the proving key are read-only, so that's safe across threads. The output arrays write to different indices, so no data races there. The tricky part is CUDA buffer allocation — but if I pre-allocate the buffers before spawning threads, each thread just uses its pre-allocated buffer without runtime allocation overhead. This should save 20-40ms by overlapping the evaluation paths.\n\nBut there's a constraint I'm hitting: the `evaluate_logup_batched` function signature...\n\nActually, I realize the mutable reference issue is a blocker. Both `logup_out` and `zc_out` are separate arrays, but Rust won't let me pass mutable references to different parts of the same structure across threads without unsafe code or `split_at_mut`. A cleaner approach would be to skip threads entirely and use CUDA streams directly — launch logup kernels on one stream and zerocheck kernels on another from the same thread. This sidesteps the aliasing problem while still getting the parallelism I need.\n\nThe catch is that the existing batch evaluation functions rely on `cudaStreamPerThread`, which is tied to the calling thread. To use explicit streams, I'd need to either modify all the CUDA wrapper functions to accept a stream parameter (which is invasive), use OS threads so each gets its own default stream, or manually create streams and call `cudaLaunchKernel` with explicit stream arguments. The OS threads approach is the most straightforward given the current codebase.\n\nSo I'll spawn two OS threads: one handles logup evaluation writing to its own output buffers, and the other handles zerocheck evaluation writing to separate data structures. Since these outputs don't overlap, I can use `std::thread::scope` to manage them cleanly.\n\nNow I'm thinking about what else can be parallelized within each MLE round. The current pipeline has several GPU stages that might overlap — sumcheck polynomial batch evaluation includes metadata collection, interpolation, and then separate evaluation phases for logup and zerocheck. Let me map out which of these could run concurrently.\n\nThe interpolation step produces values needed downstream, so logup and zerocheck evaluations depend on it finishing first. But the late evaluation phase, which processes completed traces, writes to different buffers than the early evaluation phases. That means I could potentially run late evaluation logup, early evaluation logup, and early evaluation zerocheck all at the same time without conflicts.\n\nThe bottleneck shifts depending on the round: early rounds have many traces still in progress so early evaluation dominates, while late rounds have fewer traces left but the late evaluation itself is just fast scalar operations. The real win would be parallelizing the early logup and zerocheck work since that's where most of the computation lives.\n\nI'm estimating this could save 20-40ms by running those two evaluations concurrently, bringing the total MLE round time down from 158ms to around 118-138ms. The main concern is whether the GPU can actually handle both workloads in parallel—but at APC 300 with small traces, individual kernels are small enough that they shouldn't saturate the GPU, so concurrent execution should be feasible. Memory allocation from different streams should also be fine with cudaMallocAsync's pool allocator handling concurrent requests efficiently.\n\nThe real challenge is restructuring the code to spawn and manage these concurrent threads properly, handling errors from both paths, and making sure the data transfer back to host happens efficiently—since both streams can sync independently, the total D2H time becomes the maximum of the two rather than their sum.\n\nBefore committing to this approach, I need to verify that the two evaluation functions don't share any mutable state. They both read from the proving key, access different trace indices, and write to separate output buffers, so there shouldn't be any conflicts there.\n\nNow I'm ready to formalize this as a concrete optimization: running the logup and zerocheck evaluation paths in parallel on separate CUDA streams to overlap their kernel execution and reduce overall latency. the edge. Let me see if I can combine this with other improvements.\n\nWhat if I also overlap the interpolation with the evaluation? The interpolation produces the column values that evaluation needs, so there's a dependency there. But once the interpolation kernel finishes, all values are ready, and I could immediately spawn the logup and zerocheck evaluations on separate streams. That's already what would happen naturally.\n\nNow I'm thinking about the fold operation—it takes about 3-4ms per round. Could I pipeline it with the metadata collection from the next round instead of waiting for evaluation to fully complete?\n\nThe metadata collection is pure CPU work that categ