iminating ~300 kernel launch overheads per segment.","+Overlap the logup and zerocheck evaluation paths within each MLE sumcheck round using OS threads with per-thread CUDA streams. Currently `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` runs logup monomial/batch_mle evaluation and zerocheck monomial/batch_mle/par-Y evaluation sequentially on the default CUDA stream. These paths are independent (separate input combination data, separate output arrays) and can execute concurrently on different CUDA streams, following the same multi-stream pattern used successfully for Round 0 (3.28x) and GKR input eval (1.67x). At APC 300, individual MLE kernels are small (10-500µs) and don't saturate the GPU, leaving SM capacity for concurrent execution from a second stream."]}],"originalFile":"# 2026-04-15-0100-batch-global-gkr-input-eval\n\nReplace per-AIR GLOBAL-mode GKR input evaluation kernel launches with a single batched descriptor-array kernel for small AIRs (height ≤ TASK_SIZE). Currently each of ~300 GLOBAL AIRs at APC 300 launches a 256-block kernel with TASK_SIZE=65536 threads regardless of actual height, wasting 90-99% of threads for small AIRs and serializing execution across 8 streams. A batched kernel with per-AIR descriptors processes all small GLOBAL AIRs in one launch with right-sized block counts, enabling GPU-native block scheduling instead of coarse-grained stream scheduling, and eliminating ~300 kernel launch overheads per segment.\n"}}
