{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PptNJ6BUhcvvZdeqcefiUL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XSm5yVkg8sSSduxxuGsHi2","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-15-0430-multistream-mle-round-eval/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Multi-stream MLE Round Evaluation\n\n## Goal\n\nOverlap the logup and zerocheck evaluation paths within each MLE sumcheck round using OS threads with per-thread CUDA streams (cudaStreamPerThread). This follows the same multi-stream pattern used successfully for Round 0 (3.28x improvement) and GKR input eval (1.67x improvement).\n\nThe target is the `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` function in `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs` (lines 1576-1974). Currently, within each MLE round, the function evaluates:\n1. Late logup traces (lines 1846-1865) — writes `logup_tilde_evals`\n2. Late zerocheck traces (lines 1867-1884) — writes `zerocheck_tilde_evals`\n3. Early logup traces via `evaluate_logup_batched` (lines 1888-1900) — writes `logup_out`\n4. Early zerocheck DAG traces (lines 1919-1930) — writes `zc_out`\n5. Early zerocheck par-Y traces (lines 1933-1953) — writes `zc_out`\n6. Early zerocheck monomial traces (lines 1957-1970) — writes `zc_out`\n\nAll six steps run sequentially on the calling thread's default CUDA stream. Steps 1+3 (logup) and steps 2+4+5+6 (zerocheck) are independent: they read shared immutable data (proving key, trace metadata, pre-computed combinations) and write to non-overlapping output arrays.\n\nAt APC 300, the MLE Rounds wall-clock is 158ms with ~113ms GPU kernel time and ~45ms CPU/sync overhead across 14 rounds. Per round, the logup path takes ~3ms and the zerocheck path takes ~2-3ms. With multi-streaming, the GPU work from both paths can overlap, reducing per-round eval time from ~5-6ms to ~3ms (the slower path). Expected savings: 20-40ms total.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Entry: MLE round loop\n`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs` lines 660-677:\n```\nfor round in 1..=n_max:\n    sp_round_evals = prover.sumcheck_polys_batch_eval(round, r[round - 1])\n    batch_s = prover.compute_batch_s_poly(sp_round_evals, ...)\n    // transcript observe/sample\n    prover.fold_mle_evals(round, r_round)\n```\n\n### sumcheck_polys_batch_eval (lines 1576-1974)\n1. **Phase 1** (lines 1591-1841): CPU metadata collection. Iterates all traces, splits into late_eval (Case A, round > n_lift) and early_eval (Case B, round <= n_lift). For early_eval, collects interpolation metadata, then launches a single batched interpolation kernel (`batched_interpolate_columns_matrix_kernel`).\n\n2. **Phase 2** (lines 1843-1971): GPU evaluation. Six sequential evaluation steps (listed in Goal), each creating a batch object (`LogupMonomialBatch`, `ZerocheckMonomialBatch`, etc.), launching GPU kernels, calling `to_host()` for D2H + stream sync, then processing results on CPU.\n\n### Key data structures accessed by both paths\n- `self.pk: &DeviceMultiStarkProvingKey` — read-only proving key (shared)\n- `self.logup_combinations: Vec<Option<LogupCombinations>>` — read-only, used by logup path\n- `self.lambda_combinations: Vec<Option<DeviceBuffer<EF>>>` — read-only, used by zerocheck path\n- `self.lambda_pows: Option<DeviceBuffer<EF>>` — read-only, used by DAG zerocheck path\n- `self.d_challenges: DeviceBuffer<EF>` — read-only, used by logup batch_mle path\n- `logup_out: Vec<[Vec<EF>; 2]>` — mutable, only written by logup path\n- `zc_out: Vec<Vec<EF>>` — mutable, only written by zerocheck path\n- `self.logup_tilde_evals: Vec<[EF; 2]>` — mutable, only written by logup path (late traces)\n- `self.zerocheck_tilde_evals: Vec<EF>` — mutable, only written by zerocheck path (late traces)\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: Extract evaluation into two independent closures\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n**Function**: `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`\n\nAfter Phase 1 (interpolation, line ~1841), restructure Phase 2 into two closures:\n\n**Logup closure** (runs on thread A):\n- Late logup eval (current lines 1846-1865)\n- Early logup eval via `evaluate_logup_batched` (current lines 1888-1900)\n- Returns: updates to `logup_out` and `logup_tilde_evals`\n\n**Zerocheck closure** (runs on thread B):\n- Late zerocheck eval (current lines 1867-1884)\n- Early zerocheck DAG eval (current lines 1919-1930)\n- Early zerocheck par-Y eval (current lines 1933-1953)\n- Early zerocheck monomial eval (current lines 1957-1970)\n- Returns: updates to `zc_out` and `zerocheck_tilde_evals`\n\n### Change 2: Spawn threads using std::thread::scope\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n**Function**: `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`\n\nUse `std::thread::scope` (same pattern as Round 0 multi-stream at line ~1341) to spawn the logup and zerocheck closures on separate OS threads. Each thread gets its own CUDA stream via `cudaStreamPerThread`.\n\nBorrow splitting: extract mutable references to the non-overlapping output fields before the scope:\n```rust\nlet logup_tilde_evals = &mut self.logup_tilde_evals;\nlet zerocheck_tilde_evals = &mut self.zerocheck_tilde_evals;\n```\n\nShared immutable references (pk, combinations, challenges, etc.) are captured by the closures without conflict.\n\n### Change 3: Add multi-stream threshold\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n\nOnly use multi-stream when the number of early_eval traces exceeds a threshold (e.g., `early_eval.len() >= 100`). Below the threshold, the single-stream path executes as before. This avoids thread spawn overhead when there are few small AIRs (APC 0: ~20 traces, single-thread is optimal).\n\n### Change 4: Thread-safe data passing\n\nThe closures need to capture references that are `Send`:\n- `&[TraceCtx]` (late_eval, early_eval slices) — `TraceCtx` contains raw CUDA device pointers (`*const EF`, etc.). Raw pointers are not `Send`. We need to wrap the closure references to assert thread-safety.\n- Use `unsafe impl Send` on a wrapper struct, following the pattern in `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs` where `WorkItem` with raw pointers is passed to threads (the pointers reference GPU memory that is valid across all CUDA streams on the same device).\n\nDefine a helper struct:\n```rust\nstruct EvalWork<'a> {\n    traces: Vec<&'a TraceCtx>,\n    // ... other needed refs\n}\n// SAFETY: CUDA device pointers in TraceCtx are valid on all streams\n// on the same GPU device. No host-side aliasing.\nunsafe impl<'a> Send for EvalWork<'a> {}\n```\n\n### Change 5: Error handling\n\nBoth threads return `Result<(), LogupZerocheckError>`. After joining, propagate the first error (if any). Use the same pattern as Round 0 thread error handling: collect join handles, unwrap each, return first Err.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Correctness**: The logup and zerocheck evaluation paths are mathematically independent — they compute different parts of the sumcheck polynomial (logup numerator/denominator contributions vs zerocheck constraint contributions). The final `compute_batch_s_poly` combines both results. The multi-stream change only affects execution ordering, not computation.\n\n2. **No write aliasing**: The logup path writes only to `logup_out[trace_idx]` and `logup_tilde_evals[trace_idx]`. The zerocheck path writes only to `zc_out[trace_idx]` and `zerocheck_tilde_evals[trace_idx]`. These are separate Vec allocations. Even when both paths process the same trace_idx, they write to different arrays.\n\n3. **CUDA stream safety**: Each OS thread uses `cudaStreamPerThread`, giving it a dedicated CUDA stream. GPU kernel launches and D2H copies on different streams are independent. CUDA guarantees that non-overlapping device memory writes from different streams are safe.\n\n4. **APC 0 non-regression**: The threshold guard (Change 3) ensures the single-stream path is used when there are few traces, matching current behavior exactly. Thread spawn overhead (~10-50µs) is only paid when there are enough traces to benefit from parallelism.\n\n5. **Interpolation completion**: The batched interpolation kernel (Phase 1) must complete before either evaluation path reads the interpolated data. Since the interpolation runs on the calling thread's stream BEFORE the thread scope begins, it is guaranteed to be complete (the interpolation result's `to_host()` call syncs the stream, and the interpolated data is in host memory passed by reference to both threads... wait, actually the evaluation paths read from DEVICE matrices, not host). Let me re-check.\n\n   Actually, the interpolation writes to a device buffer, and the evaluation kernels read from it. The interpolation runs on the default stream. When we spawn new threads, their streams are independent. We need to ensure the interpolation is complete before the evaluation threads read the interpolated data. Solution: call `current_stream_sync()` after the interpolation kernel and before spawning threads. This ensures the interpolated device buffer is fully written.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\nRun the standard benchmark:\n```bash\ncd /home/georg/powdr\nAUTOPRECOMPILE_COUNT=300 openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh\nAUTOPRECOMPILE_COUNT=0 openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh\n```\n\nAnalyze with:\n```bash\npython3 /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc300/metrics.json after_apc300\npython3 /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc000/metrics.json after_apc000\n```\n\nSuccess criteria:\n- MLE Rounds at APC 300 improves by at least 15ms (158ms → ≤143ms)\n- STARK excl trace at APC 300 improves by at least 15ms (1297ms → ≤1282ms)\n- No regression at APC 0 (STARK excl trace ≤ 2160ms, within noise of 2142ms)\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\nRevert if:\n- MLE Rounds improvement at APC 300 is less than 15ms\n- OR any APC 0 metric regresses by more than 20ms\n- OR proof verification fails at any APC configuration\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2265,"cache_read_input_tokens":168451,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2265},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5bb7d674-211a-472f-a536-7708d797558d","uuid":"118f4b9c-3c6e-401c-8add-9a067b87dfc8"}
