{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WoPeDvQn2y1AjLCFPbKCXY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Multi-stream MLE Round Evaluation\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tOverlap the logup and zerocheck evaluation paths within each MLE sumcheck round using OS threads with per-thread CUDA streams (cudaStreamPerThread). This follows the same multi-stream pattern used successfully for Round 0 (3.28x improvement) and GKR input eval (1.67x improvement).\n6\t\n7\tThe target is the `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` function in `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs` (lines 1576-1974). Currently, within each MLE round, the function evaluates six sequential steps:\n8\t1. Late logup traces (lines 1846-1865) — writes `logup_tilde_evals`\n9\t2. Late zerocheck traces (lines 1867-1884) — writes `zerocheck_tilde_evals`\n10\t3. Early logup traces via `evaluate_logup_batched` (lines 1888-1900) — writes `logup_out`\n11\t4. Early zerocheck DAG traces (lines 1919-1930) — writes `zc_out`\n12\t5. Early zerocheck par-Y traces (lines 1933-1953) — writes `zc_out`\n13\t6. Early zerocheck monomial traces (lines 1957-1970) — writes `zc_out`\n14\t\n15\tAll six steps run sequentially on the calling thread's CUDA stream. Steps 1+3 (logup) and steps 2+4+5+6 (zerocheck) are independent: they read shared immutable data and write to disjoint output arrays.\n16\t\n17\t## Current Code Path\n18\t\n19\t### Entry: MLE round loop\n20\t`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs` lines 660-677:\n21\t```\n22\tfor round in 1..=n_max:\n23\t    sp_round_evals = prover.sumcheck_polys_batch_eval(round, r[round - 1])\n24\t    batch_s = prover.compute_batch_s_poly(sp_round_evals, ...)\n25\t    // transcript observe/sample\n26\t    prover.fold_mle_evals(round, r_round)\n27\t```\n28\t\n29\t### sumcheck_polys_batch_eval (lines 1576-1974)\n30\t**Phase 1** (lines 1591-1841): CPU metadata collection. Iterates all traces, splits into late_eval and early_eval. For early_eval, collects interpolation metadata and launches batched interpolation kernel (`batched_interpolate_columns_matrix_kernel`). Constructs `TraceCtx` structs pointing to interpolated device buffers.\n31\t\n32\t**Phase 2** (lines 1843-1971): GPU evaluation. Six sequential steps (listed above), each creating batch objects, launching GPU kernels, copying results D2H, and processing on CPU.\n33\t\n34\t### Key data accessed by both paths\n35\t**Read-only (shared)**: `self.pk`, `self.logup_combinations`, `self.lambda_combinations`, `self.lambda_pows`, `self.d_challenges`, `self.monomial_num_y_threshold`, `self.memory_limit_bytes`, `self.sm_count`\n36\t\n37\t**Logup-only mutable**: `logup_out: Vec<[Vec<EF>; 2]>`, `self.logup_tilde_evals: Vec<[EF; 2]>`\n38\t\n39\t**Zerocheck-only mutable**: `zc_out: Vec<Vec<EF>>`, `self.zerocheck_tilde_evals: Vec<EF>`\n40\t\n41\t### CUDA synchronization primitives\n42\t- `to_host()` (`crates/cuda-common/src/copy.rs` line 102): Acquires global `COPY_EVENT` mutex, calls `cudaMemcpyAsync` + `record_and_wait(cudaStreamPerThread)`. Blocks until the per-thread stream completes all prior work.\n43\t- `to_host_on_current_stream()` (`crates/cuda-common/src/copy.rs` line 134): Mutex-free. Calls `cudaMemcpyAsync` + `current_stream_sync()`. Safe to call from multiple threads without contention.\n44\t- `MEMORY_MANAGER` (`crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/mod.rs` line 30): Global mutex protecting `d_malloc`/`d_free`. Each `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity()` and `Drop` acquires this. Hold time per acquisition: ~5-10µs (one `cudaMallocAsync` call + bookkeeping).\n45\t\n46\t## Changes\n47\t\n48\t### Change 1: Synchronize interpolation before thread spawn\n49\t\n50\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n51\t**Location**: After the interpolation kernel launch (line ~1773) and Phase 3 TraceCtx construction (line ~1841), immediately before Phase 2 evaluation begins.\n52\t\n53\tAdd `current_stream_sync()` call. This ensures the interpolated device buffer is fully written before the spawned evaluation threads read from it on their per-thread streams. Without this, the spawned threads' CUDA streams have no ordering guarantee relative to the calling thread's stream where the interpolation kernel was launched.\n54\t\n55\t```rust\n56\t// After Phase 1 (interpolation + TraceCtx construction):\n57\tcurrent_stream_sync().map_err(MemCopyError::from)?;\n58\t// Phase 2: multi-stream evaluation begins\n59\t```\n60\t\n61\t### Change 2: Extract all self borrows before thread scope\n62\t\n63\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n64\t**Function**: `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`, at the start of Phase 2\n65\t\n66\tRust's borrow checker requires explicit field-level borrow splitting when passing mutable references to different fields into separate closures. Extract ALL needed references before the `std::thread::scope`:\n67\t\n68\t```rust\n69\t// Immutable refs (shared by both threads)\n70\tlet pk = self.pk;\n71\tlet logup_combinations = &self.logup_combinations;\n72\tlet lambda_combinations = &self.lambda_combinations;\n73\tlet lambda_pows = self.lambda_pows.as_ref();\n74\tlet d_challenges_ptr = self.d_challenges.as_ptr();\n75\tlet monomial_num_y_threshold = self.monomial_num_y_threshold;\n76\tlet memory_limit_bytes = self.memory_limit_bytes;\n77\tlet sm_count = self.sm_count;\n78\t\n79\t// Mutable refs (disjoint, one per thread)\n80\tlet logup_tilde_evals = &mut self.logup_tilde_evals;\n81\tlet zerocheck_tilde_evals = &mut self.zerocheck_tilde_evals;\n82\t```\n83\t\n84\t### Change 3: Use to_host_on_current_stream in spawned threads\n85\t\n86\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n87\t\n88\tAll `to_host()` calls within the spawned evaluation closures must be replaced with `to_host_on_current_stream()`. This avoids the global `COPY_EVENT` mutex that would otherwise serialize D2H copies across threads.\n89\t\n90\tAffected call sites:\n91\t- Logup thread: inside `LogupMonomialBatch::evaluate` result (line 1861 equivalent), inside `evaluate_logup_batched` (batch_mle.rs lines 528, 609)\n92\t- Zerocheck thread: after `ZerocheckMonomialBatch::evaluate` (line 1880 equivalent), after `ZerocheckMonomialParYBatch::evaluate` (line 1950), after `ZerocheckMonomialBatch::evaluate` for low traces (line 1967), inside `evaluate_zerocheck_batched`\n93\t\n94\tImplementation approach: The batch evaluate functions (`LogupMonomialBatch::evaluate`, `ZerocheckMonomialBatch::evaluate`, etc.) return `DeviceBuffer` objects. The `to_host()` is called on these return values in the caller. Change these caller-side `to_host()` calls to `to_host_on_current_stream()`.\n95\t\n96\tFor `evaluate_logup_batched` and `evaluate_zerocheck_batched` (which internally call `to_host()` on intermediate results), add a `use_stream_sync: bool` parameter or refactor to return DeviceBuffers that the caller converts with `to_host_on_current_stream()`. The simplest approach: replace the internal `to_host()` calls in these functions with `to_host_on_current_stream()` unconditionally — `to_host_on_current_stream` works correctly in single-threaded mode too (it's just slightly different sync mechanism, but equally correct).\n97\t\n98\t### Change 4: Thread-safe wrapper for TraceCtx\n99\t\n100\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs` or `batch_mle.rs`\n101\t\n102\t`TraceCtx` contains raw CUDA device pointers (`*const EF`, etc.) which are not `Send`. The logup and zerocheck closures capture `Vec<&TraceCtx>` references.\n103\t\n104\tFollowing the pattern from `gkr_input.rs` (GKR input multi-stream): wrap the closure data in a struct that asserts `Send`:\n105\t\n106\t```rust\n107\tstruct LogupEvalWork<'a> {\n108\t    late_traces: Vec<&'a TraceCtx>,\n109\t    early_traces: &'a [TraceCtx],\n110\t    // ... other needed refs\n111\t}\n112\t// SAFETY: CUDA device pointers in TraceCtx reference GPU global memory\n113\t// that is accessible from any CUDA stream on the same device.\n114\tunsafe impl<'a> Send for LogupEvalWork<'a> {}\n115\t```\n116\t\n117\t### Change 5: Multi-stream dispatch with threshold\n118\t\n119\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n120\t\n121\tOnly use multi-stream when `early_eval.len() >= MIN_MLE_MULTISTREAM_TRACES` (set to 50). Below the threshold, run the existing sequential path. At APC 300 seg0 there are ~350 early traces per round (well above threshold). At APC 0, there are ~20 traces per round (below threshold, single-stream).\n122\t\n123\t```rust\n124\tconst MIN_MLE_MULTISTREAM_TRACES: usize = 50;\n125\t\n126\tif early_eval.len() >= MIN_MLE_MULTISTREAM_TRACES {\n127\t    // Multi-stream path: spawn logup + zerocheck on separate threads\n128\t    std::thread::scope(|s| {\n129\t        let logup_handle = s.spawn(|| { /* logup closure */ });\n130\t        let zerocheck_handle = s.spawn(|| { /* zerocheck closure */ });\n131\t        let logup_result = logup_handle.join().unwrap()?;\n132\t        let zerocheck_result = zerocheck_handle.join().unwrap()?;\n133\t        Ok(())\n134\t    })?;\n135\t} else {\n136\t    // Single-stream path: existing sequential code\n137\t    // ... (steps 1-6 unchanged)\n138\t}\n139\t```\n140\t\n141\t### Change 6: Replace to_host with to_host_on_current_stream globally in evaluation functions\n142\t\n143\t**Files**:\n144\t- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs` lines 528, 609 (inside `evaluate_logup_batched`)\n145\t- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs` (inside `evaluate_zerocheck_batched` if it exists)\n146\t\n147\tReplace all `to_host()` calls with `to_host_on_current_stream()` in the batch evaluation helper functions. This is safe in single-threaded mode (same synchronization semantics as `to_host()` — both ensure the copy is complete before returning) and eliminates COPY_EVENT mutex contention in multi-threaded mode.\n148\t\n149\tThe `to_host_on_current_stream` trait (`MemCopyD2HStreamSync`) needs to be imported wherever it replaces `to_host`.\n150\t\n151\t### MEMORY_MANAGER mutex assessment\n152\t\n153\tThe MEMORY_MANAGER mutex (`crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/mod.rs` line 30) serializes all GPU buffer allocations/frees. Each evaluation thread performs ~2-4 allocations per round (batch constructor descriptor uploads + evaluate tmp/output buffers) and ~2-4 frees (buffer Drops). Total per round: ~8-16 mutex acquisitions across both threads, at ~6µs hold time each = ~48-96µs contention per round. Over 14 rounds: ~0.7-1.3ms.\n154\t\n155\tThis is negligible compared to the ~3ms per-round kernel time being overlapped. The failed `multistream-stacked-reduction-round0` task had 112 buffers per segment (14x more allocations), which is why it caused significant contention. Our allocation count is well within acceptable limits.\n156\t\n157\tIf measured contention exceeds expectations, pre-allocating tmp_sums and output buffers before the thread scope (passing pre-allocated DeviceBuffers to evaluate via new `evaluate_into` methods) would eliminate allocation-time contention entirely. This is a fallback, not required for the initial prototype.\n158\t\n159\t## Invariants\n160\t\n161\t1. **Correctness**: Logup and zerocheck evaluation paths compute independent parts of the sumcheck polynomial. The logup path evaluates interaction (bus) contributions; the zerocheck path evaluates constraint contributions. `compute_batch_s_poly` (line 1977) combines both. Multi-streaming changes execution order, not computation.\n162\t\n163\t2. **No write aliasing**: Logup writes to `logup_out[trace_idx]` and `logup_tilde_evals[trace_idx]`. Zerocheck writes to `zc_out[trace_idx]` and `zerocheck_tilde_evals[trace_idx]`. These are separate `Vec` allocations with distinct base pointers. Even for the same trace_idx, the writes target different arrays.\n164\t\n165\t3. **Interpolation completion**: `current_stream_sync()` after the interpolation kernel (Change 1) ensures the interpolated device buffer is globally visible before evaluation threads launch kernels reading from it on their per-thread streams.\n166\t\n167\t4. **APC 0 non-regression**: Threshold guard (Change 5) ensures the sequential path is used when `early_eval.len() < 50`, matching current behavior.\n168\t\n169\t5. **D2H correctness**: `to_host_on_current_stream()` syncs the calling thread's per-thread stream, which is the same stream the kernel was launched on. This correctly ensures the kernel output is ready before the D2H copy completes.\n170\t\n171\t## Measurement Plan\n172\t\n173\t```bash\n174\tcd /home/georg/powdr\n175\tAUTOPRECOMPILE_COUNT=300 openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh\n176\tAUTOPRECOMPILE_COUNT=0 openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh\n177\tpython3 /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc300/metrics.json after_apc300\n178\tpython3 /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc000/metrics.json after_apc000\n179\t```\n180\t\n181\tSuccess criteria:\n182\t- MLE Rounds at APC 300 improves by at least 10ms (158ms → ≤148ms)\n183\t- STARK excl trace at APC 300 improves by at least 10ms (1297ms → ≤1287ms)\n184\t- No regression at APC 0 (STARK excl trace ≤ 2160ms)\n185\t\n186\t## Rollback Criteria\n187\t\n188\tRevert if:\n189\t- MLE Rounds improvement at APC 300 is less than 10ms\n190\t- OR any APC 0 metric regresses by more than 20ms\n191\t- OR proof verification fails at any APC configuration\n192\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01J7JmRX3Q7dxkz9uS1mDyLK","session_id":"5bb7d674-211a-472f-a536-7708d797558d","uuid":"e9f67886-4c85-4a01-a86f-eeadd4f97d0a","timestamp":"2026-04-15T02:59:07.927Z"}
